prompt:
You are a formal verification assistant. The following Dafny program fails Dafny verification.

=== PROGRAM ===
method GenericMax<A>(cmp: (A, A) -> bool, a: array<A>) returns (max: A)
  requires a != null && a.Length > 0
  requires forall x, y :: cmp.requires(x, y)
  requires forall x, y :: cmp(x, y) || cmp(y, x);

  ensures forall x :: 0 <= x < a.Length ==>
    // uncommenting the following line causes the program to verify
    // assume cmp.requires(a[x], max);
    cmp(a[x], max)
{
  max := a[0];
  var i := 0;
  while i < a.Length
    invariant 0 <= i <= a.Length
    invariant forall x :: 0 <= x < i ==> cmp(a[x], max)
  {
    if !cmp(a[i], max) {
      max := a[i];
    }
    i := i + 1;
  }
}

=== DAFNY ERROR ===
program.dfy(15,14): Error: this invariant could not be proved to be maintained by the loop
 Related message: loop invariant violation

Dafny program verifier finished with 1 verified, 1 error

=== COUNTEREXAMPLE ===
At initial state: a.Length = 2440. At a program state: a.Length = 2440. At a program state: i = 0.

Analyze the failure and respond with ONLY a valid JSON object — no explanation, no markdown, no code fences. Use exactly this structure:

{
  "spec_element": "<one of: requires | invariant | ensures>",
  "proof_obligation": "<one of: initiation | preservation | bridge>",
  "diagnosis": "<explanation of what broke and why>",
  "repair": "<the corrected Dafny specification line that restores the intended behavior>"
}

Definitions:
- requires: the precondition that must hold before the method executes
- invariant: the loop invariant that must hold throughout the loop
- ensures: the postcondition that the method must guarantee on return
- initiation: the precondition does not establish the invariant at loop entry
- preservation: the loop body does not maintain the invariant
- bridge: the invariant and loop exit condition do not imply the postcondition

raw_response:
{
  "spec_element": "requires",
  "proof_obligation": "preservation",
  "diagnosis": "The invariant forall x :: 0 <= x < i ==> cmp(a[x], max) cannot be shown to survive an update of max. When !cmp(a[i], max) holds, the body sets max := a[i]. Totality then gives cmp(old max, a[i]). To re-establish the invariant, the verifier must conclude cmp(a[x], a[i]) for every x < i from cmp(a[x], old max) and cmp(old max, a[i]). That step needs transitivity of cmp, and the preconditions only state totality (and that cmp is total as a function). Without transitivity the inductive step fails for an arbitrary relation, so the precondition is too weak for the intended max specification.",
  "repair": "requires forall x, y, z :: cmp(x, y) && cmp(y, z) ==> cmp(x, z)"
}
